 "raw_reply": "{\"item\": \"S446-022\",\n \"clauses\": {\n  \"divide_numbers\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F18\", \"template\": \"{dividend} ÷ {divisor} = ___\", \"reasoning\": \"F18 is a plain division task whose dividend and divisor are printed from the repeated generated values a and b; it can address the task Divide numbers.\"},\n  \"with_and_without_remainder\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F12\", \"template\": \"{q_val} R {alt_r}\", \"reasoning\": \"F12 prints a quotient with a remainder, so it addresses the with-remainder side. The without-remainder side is addressed by F11, whose template is {q_val} alone, printed as answer choices with no remainder.\"},\n  \"2_to_3_digit_numbers\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F18\", \"template\": \"{dividend} ÷ {divisor} = ___\", \"reasoning\": \"F18 prints dividends such as 12, 18, 20, 36, 46 and 100, 158, 166, 200, which are 2- and 3-digit values, with a 1-digit divisor from 2 to 4.\"},\n  \"1_digit_number_without_remainder\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F18\", \"template\": \"{dividend} ÷ {divisor} = ___\", \"reasoning\": \"F18 divisor domain is printed as 2 to 4, which are 1-digit numbers, paired with 2- and 3-digit dividends.\"},\n  \"2_digit_numbers\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F23\", \"template\": \"What is {dividend} ÷ {divisor}?\", \"reasoning\": \"F23 dividend printed runs include 12, 18, 21, 24, 36, 44 and 46 to 48, which are 2-digit numbers, with divisors 4, 6 to 7, 10, 100 and 1000.\"},\n  \"1_digit_number_with_remainder\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F18\", \"template\": \"{dividend} ÷ {divisor} = ___\", \"reasoning\": \"F18 prints 2-digit dividends such as 12, 18 and 20 with 1-digit divisors 2 to 4, which can give a remainder; the remainder is shown by the answer families F12 to F14.\"},\n  \"2_to_4_digit_numbers\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F18\", \"template\": \"{dividend} ÷ {divisor} = ___\", \"reasoning\": \"F18 dividend runs include 1000, 1010, 1420, 1500, 2000, 4000 and 9980, which are 4-digit numbers.\"},\n  \"10_100_1000\": {\"verdict\": \"PRODUCIBLE\", \"family\": \"F18\", \"template\": \"{dividend} ÷ {divisor} = ___\", \"reasoning\": \"F18 divisor is printed as 2 to 4, 10, 100, 1000, so the divisors 10, 100 and 1000 appear.\"}\n },\n \"failing_families\": [\"F05\", \"F06\", \"F07\", \"F09\", \"F10\", \"F11\", \"F12\", \"F13\", \"F14\", \"F17\", \"F18\", \"F19\", \"F21\", \"F22\", \"F23\", \"F24\", \"F25\", \"F26\"],\n \"failing_templates\": [\n  \"Ask: how many times does {b} fit into {a}? {b} × {result} = {v}.\",\n  \"Start at {a} on a number line and take equal jumps back of {b} to reach 0. How many jumps did you make? {a} ÷ {b} = ___\",\n  \"We need to divide {a} by {b}. {capitalize} is {a}; {divisor_label} is {b}.\",\n  \"{capitalize} is {result} remainder {remainder} (written as {result} R {remainder}).\",\n  \"{capitalize} of {a} ÷ {b} = {result}.\",\n  \"{q_val}\",\n  \"{q_val} R {alt_r}\",\n  \"{q_val} R {rem}\",\n  \"{v} R {rem}\",\n  \"Start at {dividend} on a number line and take equal jumps back of {divisor} to reach 0: {dividend} ÷ {divisor} = ___\",\n  \"{dividend} ÷ {divisor} = ___\",\n  \"Starting at {dividend} on a number line, taking equal jumps back of {divisor} takes {slot} jumps to reach 0\",\n  \"{actor} solved: \\\"{problem}\\\". {actor} says the {miss} is {answer}. Is {actor} correct?\",\n  \"{slot} ÷ {slot2} = {slot3}\",\n  \"What is {dividend} ÷ {divisor}?\",\n  \"{dividend} ÷ {divisor} = {fill_value}\",\n  \"{statement}. True or False?\",\n  \"What is {total} ÷ {n}?\"\n ],\n \"overall\": \"FAIL\",\n \"reasoning\": \"Each slot in F05, F07, F09, F10, F11 to F14, F17 to F19, F21 to F26 is drawn from a domain that is independent of the slots it sits beside. The dividend key a prints values up to 9980 (F18, F23, F24, F26 and others), and the divisor key b prints 2 to 4 (F18), 4 to 7 (F23), and 2 to 10 (F05, F07, F10). No domain or basis links the two, and the declared_bounds entry (table one_digit_mixed_or_power_of_ten, pure, result_unknown) is not cited by any domain. The packet therefore shows that a 4-digit dividend with a 1-digit divisor (for example 9980 with 2 to 4) can print in F18, which is outside the 2- to 3-digit by 1-digit scope and the 2- to 4-digit by 10, 100 and 1000 scope. Computed quotients from F11 to F14 and F09 inherit that range. The same independence lets a 2-digit dividend with a divisor that does not divide it print with the number-line template F06, whose template says the jumps reach 0 and which prints a = 21 and b = 8 as separate values from the observed runs; the jumps then do not reach 0 and the template's answer is a remainder, which the template does not state. F24 can print a dividend and a divisor that leave a non-whole result with the fill_value choice 6 R 4 and 7 R 5 in the same slot, and F25 wraps that statement. F21 nests F19 and F22, and its answer slot includes 2500, 3000 and 6000 as distractor candidates. Clauses are PRODUCIBLE through F18 and its neighbours, but overall the packet shows out-of-scope and misleading values in the printed domains.\"}",
